"dolphin_textedit.mst — Dolphin's OWN `UI.TextEdit` over a real EDIT control
 (DolphinDart DD10).

 `st_subclass` proved the substrate with a hand-written probe standing in for
 a control view. This is the same mechanism with the stand-in removed: a real
 `UI.TextEdit`, created by Dolphin's own `View>>create`, subclassed by
 Dolphin's own `ControlView>>subclassWindow`, and driven through Dolphin's own
 value protocol.

 The only Smalltalk here is the APP — a shell that owns a text field. That is
 what an application author writes, and from here on it is the only kind of
 Smalltalk that should be hand-written."
ShellView subclass: TextEditShell [
    | editView |

    editView [ ^editView ]

    "Build the text field as a real sub-view. `addSubView:` is what realizes
     it (`View>>basicAddSubView:` is `aView parentView: self. aView show.`),
     so the window, the subclassing and the registration all happen inside
     that one send — which is exactly how a Dolphin application builds one."
    buildEdit [
        editView := TextEdit new.
        editView parentView: self.
        editView preferredExtent: 160 @ 24.
        editView usePreferredExtent: true.
        self addSubView: editView.
        ^editView
    ]

    "Is the sub-view REALLY a Windows EDIT, or just a window we called one?
     Asked of Windows rather than of the class: `GetClassName` answers what
     the window was actually registered as, so a view that quietly fell back
     to the door's own class cannot pass."
    editClassName [
        "Dolphin's own one-argument convenience, filed onto `OS.UserLibrary`
         by the `.pax` — not a hand-rolled buffer dance here."
        ^User32 getClassName: editView handle
    ]
]
